it was one of the most daring heists in  British criminal history the gang stole  jewelry gold and cash at least 14  million pounds worth and most of it is  still missing the ring leaders were  aging villains who'd already spent many  years in jail they committed the perfect  crime  but what happened afterwards was a  disaster there have been widespread  thefts of precious stones worth hundreds  of thousands to foil the thieves headin  garden now has its own giant strong room  available to every jeweler merchant in  the district the vault was used mostly  by Hatton Garden craftsmen to store  overnight the jewelry they were working  on the gang dressed as builders and  gas-fitters struck on the night before  Good Friday one of them was careful  enough to hide his face from CCTV the  others refer to him as basil he had a  key to the main building and let the  others in through a fire escape door the  gang carried their gear down the fire  escape to the basement courtyard from  the inside basil unbolted the back door  two men climbed the stairs to the second  floor where they brought up and disabled  the lift from the ground floor one or  more of the gang dropped down the lift  shaft to the basement and forced open a  metal shutter they crawled out opened  the unlocked cupboard under the stairs  and disabled the alarm they cut the  power to the outer iron gate and slid it  open they smashed the lock off the  wooden main door and let in the others  they cut through the second iron gate  and drilled through the vault wall  concrete half a meter  the gang raided the vault twice the  first night they set off an alarm that  police didn't respond then a vital tool  broke and they had to leave and buy  another to finish the job  the ringleaders were professional  villains who made amateur mistakes Bryan  Reader 76 kept the clothes he wore on  the raid Terry Perkins 67 gave himself  away when police bugged his car John  Collins 75 used his own vehicle which  set detectives on the gangs trail Danny  Jones 60 gave his own name and address  when he bought a power tool in my view  they were very much analog criminals  operating in a digital world in a letter  to me from jail Danny Jones revealed  that he had offered to show police where  he'd hidden his loot but they had turned  down his offer you would have thought a  place would have jumped with joy but for  some reason which I don't know they are  not they're interested Jones  eventually led detectives to Edmonton  Cemetery in North London under a  gravestone they found two bags of jewels  he didn't tell them he had another  bigger stash nearby what he didn't know  police had already found that one this  part of London is the very heartland of  one of Britain's most notorious crime  organizations in criminal parlance this  is their Manor but were they involved in  the raid the police don't think so but  at least two of the Raiders were  connected to the crime Network and its  name crops up in conversations bugged by  the police 10 million pounds worth of  the stolen loot is still missing so too  is basil the mystery man for the Flying  Squad he's their most wanted suspect  Martin Brunt Sky News Hatton Garden
 

